1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,648
Your connection says online. A download is
still moving. Yet one

2
00:00:04,648 --> 00:00:09,295
app refuses to open. That sounds impossible
until you separate two

3
00:00:09,295 --> 00:00:13,943
questions: can your computer reach the
network, and does it know

4
00:00:13,943 --> 00:00:18,590
where this service lives? The second question
belongs to DNS. Let's

5
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:23,238
follow one name through a small imagined
network, then break just

6
00:00:23,238 --> 00:00:27,886
the naming part. By the end, an apparently
contradictory failure will

7
00:00:27,886 --> 00:00:32,533
make perfect sense.

8
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:37,310
We want photos.orbit.example. That name is
convenient for a person, but

9
00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:42,086
the network needs an address. Imagine a
contact name and its

10
00:00:42,086 --> 00:00:46,862
phone number: changing the number doesn't
require changing what you call

11
00:00:46,862 --> 00:00:51,638
the person. DNS provides a structured way to
find information associated

12
00:00:51,638 --> 00:00:56,414
with a name. Our made-up domain and addresses
are teaching examples.

13
00:00:56,414 --> 00:01:01,190
The blue label is the question; the green
address is the

14
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:05,967
answer we hope to receive.

15
00:01:05,967 --> 00:01:10,805
The dots have a job. Read the name from the
right:

16
00:01:10,805 --> 00:01:15,643
the root, example, orbit, then photos. Each
level belongs beneath a

17
00:01:15,643 --> 00:01:20,481
larger part of the naming tree. This
organization lets responsibility be

18
00:01:20,481 --> 00:01:25,319
divided. One organization can manage its own
branch without maintaining every

19
00:01:25,319 --> 00:01:30,157
other branch. Watch the address-like string
become a tree. We haven't

20
00:01:30,157 --> 00:01:34,995
located a machine yet; we've uncovered the
structure that will tell

21
00:01:34,995 --> 00:01:39,833
our search where to go next.

22
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:44,610
Your computer usually asks a recursive
resolver to do the search.

23
00:01:44,610 --> 00:01:49,386
Recursive describes the service being offered:
please obtain an answer for

24
00:01:49,386 --> 00:01:54,162
me. The resolver may already know something
useful. For our first

25
00:01:54,162 --> 00:01:58,938
pass, assume its relevant caches are empty.
That deliberately removes the

26
00:01:58,938 --> 00:02:03,714
shortcuts so we can see the work. The resolver
becomes our

27
00:02:03,714 --> 00:02:08,490
moving point of view, asking more specific
questions while your application

28
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:13,267
waits for one useful result.

29
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:18,105
The resolver first asks a root service about
the name. It

30
00:02:18,105 --> 00:02:22,943
doesn't receive the final photo-server
address. Instead, it receives a referral

31
00:02:22,943 --> 00:02:27,781
toward the service responsible for the next
branch. Watch the yellow

32
00:02:27,781 --> 00:02:32,619
arrow: it points to where the resolver should
ask next. A

33
00:02:32,619 --> 00:02:37,457
referral is useful knowledge, even though it
isn't the final answer.

34
00:02:37,457 --> 00:02:42,295
Think of each step as reducing the remaining
search, rather than

35
00:02:42,295 --> 00:02:47,133
each server holding the entire directory.

36
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:51,971
The next service handles our example top-level
branch. It directs the

37
00:02:51,971 --> 00:02:56,810
resolver toward the authoritative servers for
orbit.example. The search is getting

38
00:02:56,810 --> 00:03:01,648
narrower. Notice who is doing the asking: the
resolver contacts these

39
00:03:01,648 --> 00:03:06,486
services, rather than your browser personally
visiting every box. The drawing

40
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:11,324
shows logical roles, not one special physical
computer per role. Real

41
00:03:11,324 --> 00:03:16,162
DNS infrastructure can distribute each role
across many machines and locations

42
00:03:16,162 --> 00:03:21,000
while preserving this hierarchy of
responsibility.

43
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,905
Now the resolver reaches an authoritative
server for our imagined zone.

44
00:03:25,905 --> 00:03:30,810
This is where the relevant record is
maintained. In our example,

45
00:03:30,810 --> 00:03:35,714
the answer associates the photos name with
192.0.2.10. The green address

46
00:03:35,714 --> 00:03:40,619
finally appears. It's tempting to call every
earlier server unhelpful because

47
00:03:40,619 --> 00:03:45,524
it didn't know this number. But their
referrals made this last

48
00:03:45,524 --> 00:03:50,429
step possible. DNS scales by sharing
responsibility and directions, instead of

49
00:03:50,429 --> 00:03:55,333
requiring every participant to know every
answer.

50
00:03:55,333 --> 00:04:00,171
The resolver returns the address to your
computer. Keep the jobs

51
00:04:00,171 --> 00:04:05,010
separate: finding this address doesn't
download the photos, prove the application

52
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:09,848
is healthy, or promise that the destination
will respond. It supplies

53
00:04:09,848 --> 00:04:14,686
information the application needs for its next
action. Our story stops

54
00:04:14,686 --> 00:04:19,524
at that boundary. The original blue question
has become a green

55
00:04:19,524 --> 00:04:24,362
result, and the rest of the network can now be
used

56
00:04:24,362 --> 00:04:29,200
with a specific destination in mind.

57
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:34,229
Let's attach clocks to the lookup. Suppose the
local resolver exchange

58
00:04:34,229 --> 00:04:39,257
takes two milliseconds, and the three upstream
round trips take twenty,

59
00:04:39,257 --> 00:04:44,286
thirty, and forty milliseconds. These are
illustrative values, not Internet constants.

60
00:04:44,286 --> 00:04:49,314
Since each referral tells us where to ask
next, these particular

61
00:04:49,314 --> 00:04:54,343
steps happen in sequence. Add the waits: two
plus twenty plus

62
00:04:54,343 --> 00:04:59,371
thirty plus forty equals ninety-two
milliseconds. The equation simply records the

63
00:04:59,371 --> 00:05:04,400
journey we just watched, one dependent step
after another.

64
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:09,305
Now ask the same question again while the
answer is cached.

65
00:05:09,305 --> 00:05:14,210
The resolver can reply without repeating our
three upstream exchanges. In

66
00:05:14,210 --> 00:05:19,114
this simplified example, only the two-
millisecond local exchange remains. Ninety-two
divided

67
00:05:19,114 --> 00:05:24,019
by two is forty-six: this lookup is forty-six
times quicker. That

68
00:05:24,019 --> 00:05:28,924
doesn't mean the entire application becomes
forty-six times faster. We measured

69
00:05:28,924 --> 00:05:33,829
one task. Still, reusing a valid answer can
remove a surprising

70
00:05:33,829 --> 00:05:38,733
amount of repeated work from that task.

71
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:43,638
A saved answer needs an expiration rule. Our
record's time to

72
00:05:43,638 --> 00:05:48,543
live, or TTL, is sixty seconds when this cache
receives it.

73
00:05:48,543 --> 00:05:53,448
Watch the countdown shrink as the answer gets
older. Reusing it

74
00:05:53,448 --> 00:05:58,352
doesn't restart the clock. If every hit
renewed its lifetime indefinitely,

75
00:05:58,352 --> 00:06:03,257
a popular old address could remain around
forever. The TTL limits

76
00:06:03,257 --> 00:06:08,162
how long this cached copy may normally be
reused before the

77
00:06:08,162 --> 00:06:13,067
resolver needs fresh information for another
query.

78
00:06:13,067 --> 00:06:18,033
Now the authoritative record changes to
203.0.113.20. Does every cache change

79
00:06:18,033 --> 00:06:23,000
instantly? Predict the next reply before the
colors move. Our cache

80
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,967
still holds the earlier answer with thirty
seconds remaining, so it

81
00:06:27,967 --> 00:06:32,933
may keep returning that old address until its
permitted lifetime ends.

82
00:06:32,933 --> 00:06:37,900
The authority and cache can temporarily
disagree without either ignoring its

83
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:42,867
rules. Changing an authoritative record and
replacing every cached copy are

84
00:06:42,867 --> 00:06:47,833
different events, separated here by a visible
countdown.

85
00:06:47,833 --> 00:06:52,862
At zero, the saved answer is no longer fresh.
For our

86
00:06:52,862 --> 00:06:57,890
normal example, the next query triggers a new
lookup and retrieves

87
00:06:57,890 --> 00:07:02,919
the changed record. We replay the referrals to
make the refresh

88
00:07:02,919 --> 00:07:07,948
visible; real resolvers may retain other
cached information and take a

89
00:07:07,948 --> 00:07:12,976
shorter route. The important change is the
answer's freshness. The cache

90
00:07:12,976 --> 00:07:18,005
wasn't useless because it eventually expired.
Its temporary nature is what

91
00:07:18,005 --> 00:07:23,033
lets yesterday's useful shortcut give way to
today's information.

92
00:07:23,033 --> 00:07:27,938
Return to the opening puzzle. The network path
is available, but

93
00:07:27,938 --> 00:07:32,843
this resolver cannot obtain the answer it
needs. The app may

94
00:07:32,843 --> 00:07:37,748
fail before it even knows a destination
address. Another task can

95
00:07:37,748 --> 00:07:42,652
continue because it already has a destination
or a still-useful cached

96
00:07:42,652 --> 00:07:47,557
answer. That's how online and unable to open
this service can

97
00:07:47,557 --> 00:07:52,462
coexist. Real outages can have additional
causes; here, we deliberately broke

98
00:07:52,462 --> 00:07:57,367
only name resolution to isolate its
contribution.

99
00:07:57,367 --> 00:08:02,333
Three ideas now fit together. Hierarchy
divides responsibility, referrals lead the

100
00:08:02,333 --> 00:08:07,300
search, and caches save work for a limited
time. Remove hierarchy

101
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:12,267
and the directory becomes harder to
distribute. Remove caching and repeat

102
00:08:12,267 --> 00:08:17,233
questions repeat the journey. Remove
expiration and changes become harder to

103
00:08:17,233 --> 00:08:22,200
discover reliably. Follow the colored answer
rather than memorizing the box

104
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:27,167
names. Its movement, reuse, and replacement
explain the mechanism much more

105
00:08:27,167 --> 00:08:32,133
clearly than calling DNS a mysterious Internet
switch.

106
00:08:32,133 --> 00:08:37,229
A working connection and a successful name
lookup are separate requirements.

107
00:08:37,229 --> 00:08:42,324
DNS turns a readable question into useful
location information through a

108
00:08:42,324 --> 00:08:47,419
hierarchy, a resolver, and temporary memory.
The small clocks explain why

109
00:08:47,419 --> 00:08:52,514
that answer can be fast, slow, or briefly old.
So when

110
00:08:52,514 --> 00:08:57,610
one app stops while another keeps working, the
situation isn't logically

111
00:08:57,610 --> 00:09:02,705
impossible. One journey may be missing a piece
that another already

112
00:09:02,705 --> 00:09:07,800
has. The connection is alive; the directions
may be missing.
